\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The cross-entropy method (CEM) planner used with LeWorldModel (LeWM) ranks candidate action
sequences by the Euclidean distance between a predicted terminal latent and a goal latent. While
this objective succeeds on short-horizon goals, its performance degrades sharply as goals become
more temporally distant or come from a different episode. On Push-T, the planner reaches
same-episode goals 25, 50 and 100 steps ahead, and cross-episode goals, with success rates of
91/41/13/14\%, respectively. We address this degradation at long horizons and across episodes
without retraining the world model or learning a subgoal generator. We learn a temporal
distance representation over frozen latents, build a graph of the offline dataset, and select a subgoal along the graph's shortest path. A reachability critic scores predicted endpoints by expected time to goal, and latent L2 is used for the final horizon. Each component addresses a distinct limitation: the subgoal provides \emph{direction}, the critic accounts for \emph{reachability}, and the switch to L2 restores near-goal \emph{resolution}. The graph finds a route for every evaluated cross-episode pair, but reaching the goal still requires the critic.
Across goals at offsets 50 and 100, and on cross-episode goals, our method raises success from 41/13/14\% to 70/46/42\% on Push-T,
from 91/83/29\% to 98/98/66\% on Reacher, and from 34/36/16\% to 50/50/28\% on Cube. At the 25-step horizon, our method trails L2
by 2.8 points on Push-T (88.4 vs 91.2) and 1.2 points on Reacher (76.0 vs 77.2), but leads by 6.0 points on Cube (62.0 vs 56.0).
\end{abstract}

% =====================================================================================
\section{Introduction}
% \keypoints{
% \item The problem is the \emph{objective}, not the model: terminal latent L2 stops ranking
%       candidates past $\sim$50 steps and plateaus on cross-episode goals at 14\% even with a
%       5$\times$ larger budget.
% \item Existing fixes retrain the encoder, add predictors, or learn generators. We keep the
%       world model and its planner frozen and use the dataset itself as a graph.
% \item Three mechanisms, three failures: direction (graph subgoal), reachability (hitting-time
%       critic), resolution (final switch). Neither subgoal nor critic alone moves cross-episode.
% \item Cross-episode goal reaching is the headline evaluation; same-episode long horizons are
%       supporting evidence.
% \item Contributions list, four items, each tied to a table or figure.
% }

Joint-embedding world models trained from pixels \citep{lewm,dinowm,pldm} plan by search: they
sample candidate action sequences, roll each through a learned latent predictor, and select the
sequence whose predicted terminal latent lies closest to the goal latent in Euclidean (L2)
distance -- the default objective we call L2 throughout. On the horizons these
models are typically evaluated on, where the goal is a frame from the same trajectory a fixed
number of steps ahead, this objective performs well. Its performance degrades sharply, however,
as the goal becomes more temporally distant. With the frozen LeWorldModel (LeWM) checkpoint and
its published cross-entropy-method (CEM) planner, success on Push-T drops steadily as the goal
moves further ahead in time, and for goals drawn from a different episode. Giving the
planner a larger step budget does not recover this loss (\cref{tab:diagnostics}b).
In \cref{sec:problem} we show that the failure lies in the objective itself, not just in the
predictor.

Recent work targets this failure, mostly on a frozen LeWM-style model, by learning something new
on top of it \citep{sage,hilewm,hwm,ffjepa,trm,tdjepa,rcaux}. An older line of work in offline
goal-conditioned reinforcement learning instead searches the data directly, letting shortest paths
over a dataset graph select subgoals \citep{sptm,sorb,sgm,gas,ttgs,alps}, but executes them with a
learned policy or a model trained for the purpose (\cref{sec:related}). To our knowledge, no prior
method gives this graph to a frozen, pretrained pixel world model through its planning cost alone.
\citet{hilewm} point to why that would help: subgoals must remain ``data-supported, executable,
and matched to the temporal scale'' to transfer to a compact frozen world model.

We place the dataset graph inside LeWM's planner: a subgoal that is data-supported,
executable, and matched to the temporal scale by construction. We learn a temporal distance
representation (TDR)
on the frozen LeWM latents \citep{hilp,gas}, build a graph over the offline dataset in that
space, and replace LeWM's terminal cost with the distance to a subgoal -- a dataset frame reached
from the current state by the shortest path of observed transitions toward the goal, truncated to
one planning horizon. This alone meaningfully improves the mid-range regime, but does nothing for
cross-episode goals: the graph finds a route for every pair we evaluate, well inside the planning
budget, yet the planner does not execute it. The missing component is reachability, which we
address with a critic $V(\z,\z_g,h)$, the probability that the goal is reachable from latent $\z$
within $h$ steps, and score each predicted endpoint by \emph{expected hitting time} under the
steps remaining. With this term, and with the final horizon handed
back to LeWM's own objective once the goal is within one horizon, a single configuration matches or
exceeds every alternative we tested at offsets 50 and above and on cross-episode goals, across
three environments -- Push-T, Reacher, and Cube (\cref{tab:main}).

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Dataset-graph subgoals and budget-clocked reachability inside an unmodified
        planner.} Subgoals are dataset frames on a shortest path over the offline data, enabling
        cross-episode stitching; a critic scores endpoints by expected hitting time within the
        remaining budget. Without changing the world model or CEM, this improves success beyond
        one horizon and on cross-episode goals in all three environments
        (\cref{sec:method,tab:main}).
  \item \textbf{A cross-episode evaluation for frozen pixel world-model planners.} We evaluate
        goals drawn from a different episode, on fixed task pools for Push-T, Reacher and Cube.
        LeWM's planner has poor performance on such goals, and budget beyond 100 steps adds
        almost nothing (\cref{sec:setup,tab:main,tab:diagnostics}).
  \item \textbf{A component analysis.} On Push-T the graph, the critic and the final switch
        address different failures (direction, reachability, near-goal resolution), which we
        support with oracle rank correlations, hazard rates and near-miss analysis
        (\cref{sec:ablation,sec:analysis}).
\end{enumerate}

% =====================================================================================
\section{Related work}
\label{sec:related}
% \keypoints{
% \item Group by what is learned, not by year: (i) planner-side costs on a frozen model,
%       (ii) hierarchical planning with learned generators/predictors, (iii) graph search over
%       offline data with learned policies, (iv) value functions inside MPC.
% \item For each group say in one clause what we take and in one clause what we do not.
% \item Be explicit that SAGE reports higher same-episode numbers at $H\!=\!150$ and cannot stitch
%       by construction; do not hide it, it is our argument.
% }

\paragraph{Planning with latent world models.}
PlaNet and Dreamer \citep{planet,dreamerv3} plan or learn policies in a reconstruction-based
latent. DINO-WM \citep{dinowm}, V-JEPA~2-AC \citep{vjepa2} and LeWM \citep{lewm} instead plan
zero-shot with CEM in a joint-embedding latent, scoring candidates by terminal latent distance to
the goal; PLDM \citep{pldm} uses MPPI with a cost summed over the horizon. \citet{jepawm-study}
study which design choices matter for these planners.

\paragraph{Repairing the cost on a frozen model.}
TRM \citep{trm} learns a pairwise temporal cost from logged trajectories and uses it as the
terminal ranking on a frozen model; other methods retrain the encoder so that latent distance
better tracks task progress \citep{tdjepa,rcaux,dalewm,scale,valueguided,straightening}. Our TDR is
in the same family, but we use it to build a graph and pick a data-supported target rather than as
the cost, and we show the metric alone is not what helps (\cref{sec:ablation}). RC-aux's
budget-conditioned reachability \citep{rcaux} is the closest relative of our critic; ours is a
separate network with an explicit plan clock and never touches the encoder.

\paragraph{Hierarchical planning with world models.}
HWM \citep{hwm}, SAGE \citep{sage}, FF-JEPA \citep{ffjepa} and Hi-LeWM \citep{hilewm} add a learned
high-level model (coarser world models, a subgoal generator with an action prior, an action-free
planner, or macro-actions), and Hi-LeWM shows that CEM can exploit such a learned subgoal space.
Each trains on same-trajectory windows, so its subgoals are not built to lie on a route that no
single trajectory took; ours are dataset frames on a shortest path over the whole dataset, and the
cross-episode protocol is where that difference shows. SAGE reports stronger same-episode numbers
than ours at long offsets (64.7\% at $H\!=\!150$ on Push-T, against 12.7\% for LeWM alone); see
\cref{sec:limitations}.

\paragraph{Graph search over offline data.}
SPTM \citep{sptm}, SoRB \citep{sorb} and SGM \citep{sgm} build graphs over stored experience with a
learned distance, GAS \mbox{\citep{gas}} clusters a temporal distance representation \citep{hilp} into
nodes with a temporal-efficiency filter, and TTGS \citep{ttgs} runs the search at test time around
frozen policies; all hand the subgoal to a learned goal-conditioned policy. ALPS \citep{alps}, the
closest prior work, reaches Laplacian cluster-graph subgoals with CEM, but over a forward model
trained for the method and seeded by a learned behaviour prior that its results depend on. We take
GAS's graph construction essentially unchanged and hand the subgoal to a frozen, publicly released
pixel world model through its terminal cost only, with no retrained dynamics or learned action
proposals.

\paragraph{Value functions inside MPC.}
POLO \citep{polo} and TD-MPC \citep{tdmpc,tdmpc2} put a reward-trained value at the end of a short
model rollout, and goal-conditioned terminal values have been used for multi-task MPC
\citep{gcterminal}. Our critic is instead a reachability classifier trained from hindsight and
cross-episode negatives, and it enters the cost as a budget-aware expected hitting time rather
than a value.

