\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}
\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{approach}
  \caption{Graph-guided latent MPC. Training (left): We train a Critic: $V$, TDR $\psi$ and graph $G$ from the dataset frames. Planning (right): Graph identifies a subgoal. CEM scores the candidate rollouts }
  \label{fig:approach}
\end{figure}
% \keypoints{
% \item One equation per component. The reader should be able to reimplement from the section.
% \item Say what is learned (TDR: 3$\times$512 MLP; critic: 5-member MLP ensemble) and what is
%       not (encoder, predictor, CEM, graph is built not trained).
% \item Critic labels are TDR-derived (--label-source tdr); no simulator state in training.
% \item The plan clock: $h_{\mathrm{rem}}$ is the budget left \emph{after} the plan being scored.
%       This is the single most important implementation detail.
% \item Only two scalars are environment specific ($\Htd$, $\lambda$) and both are read off the
%       TDR's own step-gap calibration; $\beta=1$ everywhere.
% }

\paragraph{Frozen world model and CEM planner.}
LeWM \citep{lewm} encodes a $224\times224$ frame into a 192-d latent $\z$ with a ViT encoder $E$;
its predictor $P$ maps three past latents and three action blocks (five environment actions each,
frame-skip 5) to the next latent. Planning is CEM over $K=5$ blocks (25 environment steps) with
300 candidates, 30 iterations and 30 elites (LeWM's released configuration), scoring each candidate
by the terminal cost $c_{\mathrm{L2}}=\|\hat\z_K-\z_g\|^2$ to the encoded goal frame. The optimum is
executed for 25 steps and the planner is called again. We keep $E$, $P$ and CEM fixed and change
only the cost that scores $(\z_0,\hat\z_{1..K},\z_g)$. Beyond one horizon, L2 loses its ranking
signal (\cref{sec:problem}), so there we substitute three signals that stay informative at range:
TDR distance (\cref{sec:graph}), a subgoal on a dataset graph (\cref{sec:subgoal}), and a
reachability critic (\cref{sec:critic}). Within one horizon we keep L2 (\cref{fig:approach}).

\subsection{Temporal distance representation and dataset graph}
\label{sec:graph}
We train a temporal distance representation $\psiT:\mathbb{R}^{192}\to\mathbb{R}^{32}$ on the
encoded dataset $\mathcal{D}=\{(\z,\z')\}$ of consecutive frames by expectile TD, as in HILP and
GAS \citep{hilp,gas}. With $V(\z,\z_g)=-\|\psiT(\z)-\psiT(\z_g)\|$, an EMA target $V^-$, and TD
target $q=-1+\gamma\,V^-(\z',\z_g)$ with $\gamma=0.99$,
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{TDR}}=\mathbb{E}_{(\z,\z')\sim\mathcal{D},\,\z_g}\big[\ell_{0.99}\big(q-V(\z,\z_g)\big)\big],
  \qquad \ell_\tau(u)=|\tau-\1_{u<0}|\,u^2,
  \label{eq:tdr}
\end{equation}
with goals relabelled as in GAS (\cref{app:hyperparams}). $\|\psiT(\z)-\psiT(\z_g)\|$ then
approximates the discounted step count between $\z$ and $\z_g$; we convert TDR distance to steps
with $u_k=\mathrm{median}_t\|\psiT(\z_t)-\psiT(\z_{t+k})\|$ over same-episode pairs $k$ steps apart.

The graph follows GAS. Let $F(\z_t)$ be the first later frame of $t$'s trajectory with
$\|\psiT(F(\z_t))-\psiT(\z_t)\|\ge\Htd$ (the episode's last frame if none), and define temporal
efficiency
\begin{equation}
  \mathrm{TE}(\z_t)=\cos\angle\big(\psiT(F(\z_t))-\psiT(\z_t),\ \psiT(\z_{t+n_{\mathrm{TD}}})-\psiT(\z_t)\big),
  \label{eq:te}
\end{equation}
the agreement between where the logged trajectory moves in $n_{\mathrm{TD}}=\lfloor\Htd\rceil$
environment steps ($\Htd$ rounded to a step count, as in GAS) and the direction $\psiT$ calls
optimal. Frames with $\mathrm{TE}<\theta_{\mathrm{TE}}=0.9$ are dropped; the remainder are
clustered sequentially in $\psiT$-space -- assign to the nearest existing centre if within
$\Htd/2$, else open a new one, then reset centres to member means -- giving nodes $v\in V_G$ with
centre $c_v\in\mathbb{R}^{32}$, the mean $\psiT$ of the node's members. Nodes within $\Htd$ of each
other are joined by an edge weighted by their $\psiT$-distance, with $\Htd=u_{12}$. At planning
time the goal is attached as a temporary node linked to every node within $\max(\Htd,1.2\,d_{\min})$
($d_{\min}$ is the goal's distance to its nearest node), and one Dijkstra pass gives the cost-to-go
$D_g[v]$ from every node.

\subsection{Subgoal one horizon ahead, and the final switch}
\label{sec:subgoal}
At each planning call with current latent $\z_0$, we select the start node
\begin{equation}
  v^\star=\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{v\,:\,\|\psiT(\z_0)-c_v\|\le\Htd} D_g[v]+\|\psiT(\z_0)-c_v\|,
  \label{eq:startnode}
\end{equation}
and walk the shortest path from $v^\star$ toward the goal until the cumulative $\psiT$-distance
from $\z_0$ first reaches the lookahead $\lambda=u_{25}$, the calibrated distance of one planning
horizon; call the node reached $v_\lambda$. The base cost is
\begin{equation}
  c_{\mathrm{base}}(\hat\z_K)=
  \begin{cases}
    c_{\mathrm{L2}}(\hat\z_K) & \text{if } \|\psiT(\z_0)-\psiT(\z_g)\|\le\lambda
      \text{ or the walk reaches }\z_g,\\[2pt]
    \|\psiT(\hat\z_K)-c_{v_\lambda}\| =: c_{\mathrm{sub}}(\hat\z_K) & \text{otherwise;}
  \end{cases}
  \label{eq:subgoal}
\end{equation}
we call the first case the \emph{final switch}. \Cref{sec:ablation} shows that a one-horizon
lookahead beats shorter ones and beats tracking the intermediate latents $\hat\z_{1..K-1}$, and
that the switch is needed because a node's radius ($\Htd/2$) is comparable to the success
tolerance, while L2 is still discriminative at that range (\cref{app:predictor-error}).

\subsection{Budget-clocked expected hitting time}
\label{sec:critic}
A reachability critic $V(\z,\z_g,h)\in[0,1]$, a five-member MLP ensemble over $(\z,\z_g,h)$ with
$h$ on a grid of multiples of 5 up to $h_{\max}=50$, estimates the probability that the goal is
reached from $\z$ within $h$ environment steps. It is trained on five terms:
\begin{align}
  \text{(i) hindsight:}\quad & y=\1_{h\ge\tau}; \notag\\
  \text{(ii) cross-episode:}\quad & y=0\ \text{if}\ \|\psiT(\z)-\psiT(\z_g)\|\ge 1.25\,u_{\max(h,5)}; \notag\\
  \text{(iii) imagined:}\quad & \text{predictor rollout under logged actions, labelled as in (i)};
    \notag\\
  \text{(iv) Bellman:}\quad & q=\max_{m\le M}\big(\bar V(\hat\z'_m,\z_g,h-5)-\kappa\,
    \bar\sigma(\hat\z'_m,\z_g,h-5)\big)_{[0,1]}; \notag\\
  \text{(v) monotonicity:}\quad & \max\big(0,\ V(\z,\z_g,h)-V(\z,\z_g,h+5)\big). \notag
\end{align}
Here $\tau$ is the first logged step with $\|\psiT(\z_{t+k})-\psiT(\z_g)\|<u_2$, and (ii) keeps only
cross-episode pairs that the TDR places beyond budget $h$. In (iv), $\hat\z'_m=P(\z,a_m)$ for $M$
action blocks around and away from the logged one, and $\bar V,\bar\sigma$ are the mean and standard
deviation of an EMA target ensemble. Terms (i)--(iv) enter by binary cross-entropy and (v) as a
hinge. No label reads the simulator state, so training and inference are both pixel-only; on
Push-T the TDR-derived $\tau$ agrees with the success predicate on 97.7\% of draws.

Rather than the usual $-\log V$, we score an endpoint by its expected time to the goal, capped at
the budget left after the plan being scored:
\begin{equation}
  \ET(\hat\z_K, \z_g)=5\sum_{h\in\{0,5,\dots,h_{\mathrm{rem}}\}}\big(1-V(\hat\z_K,\z_g,h)\big),
  \qquad h_{\mathrm{rem}}=\min\big(h_{\max},\,B-t-5K\big),
  \label{eq:et}
\end{equation}
where $B$ is the episode budget and $t$ the current step, kept by a \emph{plan clock} that advances
one horizon per CEM call; without it, the critic is asked about budget already spent. Feasible
endpoints are ordered by arrival time, and infeasible ones sit at the cap. Both terms are
standardised by median and interquartile range over each environment's 300 CEM candidates
(denoted $\tilde{\cdot}$) and added:
\begin{equation}
  c=\widetilde{c_{\mathrm{base}}}+\beta\,\widetilde{\ET},\qquad \beta=1.
  \label{eq:compose}
\end{equation}
\Cref{alg:main} (\cref{app:algorithm}) summarises one planning call.

\paragraph{What is trained.}
Only the TDR and the critic are trained (compute in \cref{sec:analysis}); $E$, $P$ and CEM are
LeWM's released ones and the graph rules are GAS's. Only $\Htd=u_{12}$ and $\lambda=u_{25}$ differ
by environment, read off the TDR calibration; every other value is shared (\cref{app:hyperparams}).

% =====================================================================================
\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Setup}
\label{sec:setup}
% \keypoints{
% \item Fixed task pools: 200 (start, goal) pairs per protocol per environment drawn once and shared
%       by every method and seed; a ``seed'' changes CEM's draws (and, where stated, the learned
%       assets), never the tasks. State how many of the 200 each table uses.
% \item Protocols: same-episode offset $K\in\{25,50,100,150\}$ with budget $2K$ (cap 250);
%       cross-episode with budget 250 (Push-T), 200 (Reacher), \todo{Cube}.
% \item Success is the environment's own predicate. Say it for each environment.
% \item Baselines: L2 (LeWM); L2 with more search; TDR-only cost; graph cost-to-go; oracle
%       subgoal; \todo{TRM-style learned cost; SAGE}. Random floor.
% }

\paragraph{Environments and data.}
Push-T \citep{dinowm,lewm}: the 18{,}685 expert episodes of LeWM's released training file;
success when agent and block position errors are below 20\,px and block angle error below $\pi/9$
(the environment's own success check). Reacher (dm\_control): 10{,}000 episodes of 201
frames; success when both joints are within 0.05\,rad of the goal configuration (raw difference,
no angle wrap; the dataset's shoulder angle is unwrapped over $\pm8$\,rad). OGBench Cube
\citep{ogbench}: LeWM's dataset \citep{lewm} of 10{,}000 episodes of 201 frames; success when the cube's position is within
0.04\,m of its target (\texttt{CubeEnv.\_compute\_successes}, position only, no orientation or
effector-pose requirement). Frozen LeWM
checkpoints are the public ones for each environment. 

\paragraph{Protocols.}
For a same-episode offset $K$, the goal is the frame $K$ steps after the start on the same
trajectory and the budget is $2K$ steps. For the cross-episode protocol the start and goal are
uniformly random frames of two different episodes with no distance or reachability filter and a
budget of one full episode. We use a fixed set of 50 tasks per protocol for every
method and every seed. Each seed retrains the learned assets (TDR, graph, critic) and
reseeds CEM sampling, so reported standard deviations include both sources of variation;
L2 has no learned assets, and its seeds vary CEM sampling only.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
\emph{L2} is LeWM's planner. \emph{TDR} scores endpoints by $\|\psiT(\hat\z_K)-\psiT(\z_g)\|$ with no
graph; \emph{CTG} uses the graph cost-to-go $\min_v\|\psiT(\hat\z_K)-c_v\|+D_g[v]$ as a terminal value
with no subgoal commitment. \emph{Random} takes a uniform action from the env's own action space
at every step (no model, no planning): the floor L2 and Ours must clear (\cref{tab:main}).
\emph{GCIQL} is the offline RL baseline of \citet{lewm}, trained with stable-worldmodel's released
\texttt{gciql} configuration on the frozen LeWM latents, for 10 epochs per phase, with one training
seed on Push-T only.


