On Push-T (\cref{tab:main}) the method is 2.8 points under L2 at same-ep 25 (88.4 vs 91.2),
gains 29 points at same-ep 50 (70.0 vs 40.8), and is the strongest configuration in our study at
same-ep 100 and on cross-episode goals (46.4 and 42.4 against 13.2 and 14.4). On Reacher, L2's
success rate holds up well for same-episode goals across horizons (77.2/90.8/82.8) but is poor
cross-episode (29.2); the method matches this same-episode performance (76.0/98.0/97.6) and
reaches 65.6\% cross-episode, a $+36$-point gain. At same-ep 25 on Reacher the method is 1.2
points under L2. On Cube the method wins at every protocol, including same-ep 25 (62.0 vs
56.0) where Push-T and Reacher do not: +6.0 at same-ep 25, +15.2 at same-ep 50, +14.8 at
same-ep 100, and +11.2 on cross-episode goals.

The GCIQL policy, trained on the same frozen latent and training episodes, reaches 42.0\% at
same-ep 25 on Push-T, above the random floor but 49 points below L2. It falls to 26.0 and 22.0
at same-ep 50 and 100 and solves 1 of 50 cross-episode tasks (2.0\%). A reactive goal-conditioned
policy learned offline from these data therefore does not substitute for planning with the world
model, even where the goal lies within one planning horizon.

\subsection{Ablation ladder}
\label{sec:ablation}
\Cref{tab:ablation} adds one component at a time to Push-T, on the same task pool and seeds as
\cref{tab:main}. The full ladder, including the $\beta$ and $H_{\mathrm{TD}}$ sweeps, is in
\cref{app:ablation}; the same rows on Cube are in \cref{app:ablation-cube}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering\small
\caption{Push-T ablation (success \%, same tasks and seeds as \cref{tab:main}). Rows (a)--(c)
build the graph subgoal without a critic; (d) adds the critic without a graph; (e)--(g) add a
critic to the graph subgoal; (h) scores the subgoal with LeWM's L2 instead of TDR distance.
Full ladder in \cref{tab:ladder}. \textbf{Bold}: best mean per column; \underline{underlined}:
$\pm$sd interval overlaps the best's.}
\label{tab:ablation}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}
\toprule
& configuration & same-ep 25 & same-ep 50 & same-ep 100 & cross-ep \\
\midrule
& L2 (LeWM) & \textbf{91.2} $\pm$ 1.0 & 40.8 $\pm$ 3.7 & 13.2 $\pm$ 3.2 & 14.4 $\pm$ 3.9 \\
(a) & TDR terminal cost (no graph) & 83.2 $\pm$ 4.1 & 48.4 $\pm$ 1.5 & 22.4 $\pm$ 5.3 & 14.0 $\pm$ 1.3 \\
(b) & graph subgoal & 80.8 $\pm$ 2.7 & 51.6 $\pm$ 2.3 & 22.4 $\pm$ 5.0 & 15.2 $\pm$ 3.0 \\
(c) & (b) + final switch & \underline{88.0} $\pm$ 2.5 & 62.4 $\pm$ 2.7 & 30.8 $\pm$ 6.5 & 17.2 $\pm$ 2.7 \\
\midrule
(d) & L2 + ET critic (no graph) & \underline{89.2} $\pm$ 3.0 & 58.4 $\pm$ 5.1 & \underline{38.4} $\pm$ 7.1 & 31.6 $\pm$ 2.3 \\
(e) & (b) + ET critic (no switch) & 86.0 $\pm$ 3.4 & \underline{64.8} $\pm$ 3.0 & \underline{43.6} $\pm$ 5.0 & 35.2 $\pm$ 2.0 \\
(f) & (c) + $-\log V$ critic & 77.6 $\pm$ 1.5 & \underline{69.2} $\pm$ 2.4 & \textbf{47.2} $\pm$ 5.9 & \underline{34.4} $\pm$ 7.3 \\
(g) & (c) + ET critic (\textbf{ours}) & \underline{88.4} $\pm$ 2.3 & \textbf{70.0} $\pm$ 2.2 & \underline{46.4} $\pm$ 3.2 & \textbf{42.4} $\pm$ 3.2 \\
(h) & (g), subgoal scored by L2 & 86.0 $\pm$ 2.2 & \underline{63.6} $\pm$ 4.8 & \underline{45.2} $\pm$ 3.5 & \underline{36.4} $\pm$ 2.9 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Direction: the graph subgoal.} A graph subgoal (b) lifts offset 50 and 100 over L2
(40.8 $\to$ 51.6\%, 13.2 $\to$ 22.4\%). The TDR metric alone (a) gets part of this, so the
graph's route adds to the metric, not just replaces L2's geometry. At offset 50, L2's closest
approach is 32\,px but it drifts to a final error of 76\,px; with the subgoal, closest approach
and final error are both 19\,px. The route matters more than how the subgoal is scored. In (h)
the planner follows the same graph and subgoal node, but ranks candidates by LeWM's L2 distance
to the node's medoid latent instead of TDR distance. It keeps most of the full method's gain at
offset 100 (45.2 vs.\ 46.4\%) and loses about six points at offset 50 and cross-episode
(63.6 vs.\ 70.0\%, 36.4 vs.\ 42.4\%).

\textbf{Resolution: the final switch.} Handing the last horizon back to $c_{\mathrm{L2}}$ (c)
closes most of the same-ep 25 gap (80.8 $\to$ 88.0\%) and lifts offset 50 and 100 further. On
its own it leaves cross-episode unchanged (17.2\%): resolution near the goal does not tell the
planner which candidates can reach it. With the critic in place the switch still matters.
Removing it (e vs.\ g) costs 2--7 points on every protocol, the most at cross-episode
(35.2 vs.\ 42.4\%). This is consistent with the node-radius floor: without the switch, the
planner stops short of goals the critic has already judged reachable.

\textbf{Reachability: the critic.} The critic supplies most of the cross-episode gain. On top
of L2 alone (d) it doubles cross-episode success (14.4 $\to$ 31.6\%); on top of the graph (g) it
reaches 42.4\%. The graph therefore adds 8--12 points over the critic alone at offset 50, 100
and cross-episode, and the critic adds 8--25 points over the graph alone. Without the critic the
graph finds a route but the block drifts once the approach begins (median final error 125\,px
vs.\ 26\,px with the critic).

\textbf{ET versus $-\log V$.} The two critic costs tie at long range, but $-\log V$ (f) loses
11 points at offset 25. There the critic saturates (67\% of the CEM population is feasible,
versus 39--41\% at offset 100 and cross-episode), and per-population standardisation turns that
saturation into a full vote. The capped hitting time keeps ranking feasible candidates by how
soon they arrive. With ET, a single $\beta=1$ is best or within one standard deviation of the
best on every protocol (\cref{tab:ladder}).

\subsection{Mechanistic analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}
We give three explanations for the results in \cref{tab:main}. \Cref{tab:diagnostics}
compares Push-T success, at same-ep 100 and
cross-episode, in varying conditions.

