\
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}
\toprule
window & \multicolumn{2}{c}{same-ep 100} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{cross-ep} \\
(steps) & Ours & L2 & Ours & L2 \\
\midrule
0--50    & 8.8 (8.8)   & 8.8 (8.8)  & 14.8 (14.8) & 6.0 (6.0) \\
50--100  & 23.2 (15.8) & 12.0 (3.5) & 30.4 (18.3) & 12.0 (6.4) \\
100--150 & 36.4 (17.2) & 12.4 (0.5) & 36.4 (8.6)  & 13.2 (1.4) \\
150--200 & 46.4 (15.7) & 13.2 (0.9) & 38.8 (3.8)  & 14.0 (0.9) \\
200--250 & --          & --         & 42.4 (5.9)  & 14.4 (0.5) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{minipage}
\end{table}

\paragraph{L2 ranks well only at short range; the graph degrades more gracefully.} Scoring
CEM's own first-iteration elites against the privileged recovery-time oracle
(\cref{app:cem-trace}), L2's cost is the better ranker at same-ep 25 (Spearman 0.396 vs.\ 0.327
for the graph cost) but collapses beyond it, to 0.086 at same-ep 50 and $-$0.064 at same-ep 100 --
statistically indistinguishable from chance. The graph cost degrades far more gracefully over the
same range (0.327 $\to$ 0.203 $\to$ 0.191). This is the justification for the switch to L2 within the final horizon
(\cref{eq:subgoal}): L2 is the better signal at short range, and the graph is preferred at longer horizons where L2's ranking fails.

\paragraph{Our failures are closer misses than L2's.} Loosening Push-T's success threshold
from its default 20\,px/20$^\circ$ to 25\,px/25$^\circ$ and 30\,px/30$^\circ$
(\cref{tab:diagnostics}a) recovers far more of Our failures than L2's: at same-ep 100, Ours rises 46.4 $\to$ 52.8 $\to$ 58.0\% while L2 moves only 13.2 $\to$ 14.8 $\to$ 16.0\%; at
cross-episode, Ours rises 42.4 $\to$ 51.2 $\to$ 57.6\% against L2's 14.4 $\to$ 19.2 $\to$
22.8\%. The Ours-over-L2 gap widens rather than narrows under a looser criterion, so the
advantage in \cref{tab:main} is not an artifact of the strict threshold disproportionately
penalizing Our near-misses -- when Ours fails, it still remains closer to the goal than L2 does.

\paragraph{L2 stalls after its first replan; Ours keeps converging.} Binning success by
cumulative environment-step budget (\cref{tab:diagnostics}b) shows L2's hazard rate -- the
fraction of still-unsolved tasks solved within each successive 50-step window -- collapses to
under 3.5\% after the first window at both same-ep 100 and cross-episode: nearly all of L2's
successes come from its first two plans, and additional budget shows marginal improvement
(cross-episode success rises only from 12.0\% at 100 steps to 14.4\% at 250).
Our hazard rate instead stays between 8.6\% and 18.3\% through same-ep 100's entire budget and
cross-episode's first three windows, so a substantial share of the tasks still unsolved keep
getting solved by later replans. This is explains how Our advantage in \cref{tab:main}
accumulates: it is able to maintain sustained progress at later steps, while L2 is incapable of doing so.

\paragraph{Demonstration retrieval helps Cube, but not Push-T or Reacher.} CEM starts every
plan from a zero-mean action distribution. Demonstration retrieval replaces that mean with a
logged action block taken from the training data. At each planning call, we pick the dataset
frame $i$ minimising $\|\psiT(\z_i)-\psiT(\z_0)\|+\|\psiT(\z_{i+25})-c_{v_\lambda}\|$: its
segment starts near the current state and ends near the graph subgoal (or near the goal after
the final switch). The 25 logged actions that follow $i$ become CEM's initial mean. Nothing else
changes (\cref{app:retrieval}). On Cube, retrieval adds 24.4, 9.6, 28.4 and 20.4 points to our
method at the four protocols (\cref{tab:retrieval}) and also lowers seed variance. The same
change costs 1.6--3.2 points on Push-T and 2.0--14.0 points on Reacher. On Push-T the retrieved
actions are accurate, but CEM does not need them to solve short pushes. On Reacher they are
no better than random actions, because the two-joint arm reaches similar states through
different joint motions. Cube's grasp-then-carry motions are hard for CEM to find from a
zero-mean start, and an imperfect demonstration is a large head start there. We therefore
report retrieval as a Cube-specific option, not part of the method in \cref{tab:main}.

