The ordering of components differs from Push-T. On Cube the TDR terminal cost alone (a) is the
strongest configuration on cross-episode goals (36.8\%), is within 0.8 points of the full method
at same-ep 50 (48.8 vs.\ 49.6\%), and ties it at same-ep 100 (50.4\%). Adding the graph subgoal (b) does not improve on (a) in any
protocol, and lowers cross-episode success by 8.4 points. The final switch (c) recovers 5.2 points
at same-ep 25 but costs 7.6 points at same-ep 100. The ET critic is most useful at short range:
on top of L2 alone (d) it adds 8.0 points at same-ep 25 and 10.8 at same-ep 50, but only 1.2 on
cross-episode goals, whereas on Push-T the same row doubled cross-episode success. With the
graph in place the critic changes cross-episode success by at most 4 points in either
direction ((e)--(g) against (b)). The final switch also behaves differently: on Push-T it helps at every protocol, whereas on
Cube removing it (e) gives the best result at same-ep 100 (56.8\%).

On Cube, most of the full method's gain over L2 comes from the learned temporal distance. The
TDR terminal cost alone (a) accounts for nearly all of the gain at same-ep 50 and 100 (+14.4 and
+14.8 points over L2, against +15.2 and +14.8 for the full method) and exceeds it on
cross-episode goals. The critic supplies the gain at same-ep 25, where TDR alone falls below L2.
The graph route and the final switch, which drive the Push-T results, add little on Cube. The
contribution of each component is therefore environment-dependent, and the component-wise
conclusions of \cref{sec:ablation} are specific to Push-T. The combined method is nonetheless
the most robust configuration: it is the only row that is best or within one standard deviation
of the best on every protocol in both \cref{tab:ablation} and \cref{tab:ablation-cube}.

\section{Demonstration retrieval}
\label{app:retrieval}

\paragraph{Method.} CEM's first population is sampled from $\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, I)$ in
z-scored action space, so every plan starts from ``do nothing''. Retrieval uses the dataset as an
action library to choose a better starting mean. Let $\mathcal{T}$ be the training frames whose
episode has at least $L=25$ steps left (five 5-action blocks, one planning horizon). At each
planning call with current latent $\z_0$ and target $t$, where $t=c_{v_\lambda}$ while the graph
subgoal is active and $t=\psiT(\z_g)$ after the final switch (\cref{eq:subgoal}), we select
\begin{equation}
  i^\star=\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{i\in\mathcal{T}}\ \|\psiT(\z_i)-\psiT(\z_0)\|+\|\psiT(\z_{i+L})-t\|,
  \label{eq:retrieval}
\end{equation}
by exact nearest-neighbour search on the GPU. The logged actions $a_{i^\star},\dots,a_{i^\star+L-1}$
are z-scored with the same normaliser the predictor uses, reshaped into five action blocks, and
passed to CEM as its initial mean. The variance, number of samples, elites, iterations, cost
and replanning interval are unchanged. 
\paragraph{Motivation.} Cube's action space is five-dimensional, against two dimensions on
Push-T and Reacher. With a fixed budget of 300 samples per iteration, a zero-mean CEM population
covers this larger space far more sparsely, so good action sequences are less likely to be
sampled at all. Retrieval addresses this by starting the search from a demonstrated action
block instead of from zero. We evaluate it on all five seeds and all four protocols, for all
three environments (\cref{tab:retrieval}); the retrieval flag is the only difference from our
method's runs.

\paragraph{Results.} On Cube, retrieval wins in every protocol and on every seed at
same-ep 25, same-ep 100 and cross-episode. The gain is smallest at same-ep 50 (+9.6) and largest at
same-ep 100 (+28.4) and cross-episode (+20.4). Its seed-to-seed sd is lower than our method's in
every protocol (e.g.\ 2.8 vs.\ 6.1 on cross-episode). On Push-T it loses 1.6--3.2 points in the
same-episode protocols and ties on cross-episode, with higher variance. On Reacher it loses in
every protocol, most at same-ep 25 ($-14.0$) and least on cross-episode ($-2.0$). Retrieval therefore does not help on
Push-T or Reacher, likely because in their two-dimensional action spaces the CEM population
alone already covers the space adequately, so a demonstrated starting mean adds little and can
bias the search.


\section{Live rendering must match the dataset}
\label{app:render}
The frozen encoder is sensitive to renderer versions. Reacher frames rendered with MuJoCo 3.12
lack the floor checker texture of the dataset's MuJoCo 3.5 frames; the pixel difference is 18/255
on average and moves the latent by a median of 15 units, about one 25-step gap, so the terminal
cost never reaches zero even with the arm at the goal. This alone took LeWM's own planner from 87\%
to 65\% at offset 25. 


\section{Where the latent-L2 objective fails}
\label{app:lewm-failure}
This section traces one failure mode of terminal latent L2. \Cref{app:hd-gaussian} states two
properties of high-dimensional isotropic Gaussians that the rest of the section uses.
\Cref{app:predictor-error} measures how far apart start and goal latents sit as the horizon
grows, and \cref{app:gaussian-shell} shows why a straight line between two such latents leaves
the region the encoder populates. \Cref{app:cem-trace} checks the same failure inside CEM's own
candidate ranking.

\subsection{Characteristics of high-dimensional Gaussians}
\label{app:hd-gaussian}
Let $z\sim\mathcal N(\mu,\sigma^2 I_d)$, let $\chi_d$ denote the Euclidean norm of a standard
Gaussian vector in $\mathbb R^d$, and write $m_d=\mathbb E[\chi_d]=\sqrt2\,
\Gamma(\tfrac{d+1}{2})/\Gamma(\tfrac d2)$ for the mean of a chi distribution with $d$ degrees of
freedom. By Wendel's inequality for ratios of Gamma functions \citep{wendel1948note},
\begin{equation}
  \frac{d}{\sqrt{d+1}}\;\le\; m_d\;\le\;\sqrt d .
  \label{eq:chi-mean}
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Distance between random pairs]
\label{lem:random-pair}
Let $z_1,z_2$ be independent draws of $\mathcal N(\mu,\sigma^2 I_d)$. Then
$\|z_1-z_2\|\sim\sqrt2\,\sigma\,\chi_d$, so
\[
  \mathbb E\|z_1-z_2\|=\sqrt2\,\sigma m_d\in\Big[\sqrt2\,\sigma\tfrac{d}{\sqrt{d+1}},\ \sqrt{2d}\,\sigma\Big],
  \qquad
  \operatorname{Var}\|z_1-z_2\|=2\sigma^2(d-m_d^2)\le 2\sigma^2 .
\]
In particular, $\mathbb E\|z_1-z_2\|=\sqrt2\,\mathbb E\|z-\mu\|$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$z_1-z_2$ is Gaussian with mean $0$ and covariance $2\sigma^2 I_d$, so $\|z_1-z_2\|/(\sqrt2\sigma)$
is the norm of a standard normal vector in $\mathbb R^d$, which is $\chi_d$-distributed. The
mean follows from the definition of $m_d$ and \cref{eq:chi-mean}. For the variance,
$\mathbb E[\chi_d^2]=d$, so $\operatorname{Var}[\chi_d]=d-m_d^2\le d-d^2/(d+1)=d/(d+1)<1$ by the
lower bound in \cref{eq:chi-mean}. The last claim holds because $\|z-\mu\|\sim\sigma\chi_d$.
\end{proof}

The typical distance between unrelated points therefore grows as $\sigma\sqrt{2d}$, while its
standard deviation stays below $\sqrt2\,\sigma$. At $d=192$ the ratio of standard deviation to mean is at most $\sqrt{d+1}/d<7.3\%$:
nearly all unrelated pairs are about equally far apart.

\begin{proposition}[Gaussian shell]
\label{prop:shell}
Let $r=\|z-\mu\|$ with $z\sim\mathcal N(\mu,\sigma^2 I_d)$. Then
$\mathbb E[r]=\sigma m_d\in[\sigma d/\sqrt{d+1},\ \sigma\sqrt d]$ and
$\operatorname{Var}[r]\le\sigma^2$. Moreover, for every $\varepsilon\in(0,1]$,
\[
  \Pr\big[r\le(1-\varepsilon)\,\sigma\sqrt d\,\big]\;\le\;\exp\!\big(-d\varepsilon^2/4\big).
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$r/\sigma\sim\chi_d$, so the moments follow as in \cref{lem:random-pair}. For the tail, the
lower-tail bound of \citet{laurent2000adaptive} gives
$\Pr[\chi_d^2\le d-2\sqrt{dx}]\le e^{-x}$ for all $x>0$. The event
$r\le(1-\varepsilon)\sigma\sqrt d$ is the event $\chi_d^2\le d(1-\varepsilon)^2$. Choose $x$
with $d-2\sqrt{dx}=d(1-\varepsilon)^2$, that is $\sqrt x=\sqrt d\,(2\varepsilon-\varepsilon^2)/2$.
Since $2\varepsilon-\varepsilon^2\ge\varepsilon$ on $(0,1]$, $x\ge d\varepsilon^2/4$, and the bound
follows.
\end{proof}

\Cref{prop:shell} says that the mass of a high-dimensional isotropic Gaussian lies in a thin
shell of radius about $\sigma\sqrt d$ and width $O(\sigma)$, and that the interior of the ball is
exponentially unlikely. One consequence matters for planning. If $z_1,z_2$ are independent,
their midpoint satisfies $\tfrac12(z_1+z_2)-\mu\sim\mathcal N(0,\tfrac{\sigma^2}{2}I_d)$, so by
\cref{prop:shell} it concentrates at radius about $\sigma\sqrt{d/2}$, a factor $1/\sqrt2$
inside the shell. A straight line between two typical points therefore passes through a region
that the distribution itself almost never visits.

\subsection{Predictor rollout error by horizon}
\label{app:predictor-error}
Rolling the frozen predictor forward under real logged actions and comparing to the true
encoded endpoint, Push-T's latent rollout error grows with horizon but stays well below the
scale of two unrelated frames (mean 19.6) even at $H=100$ (\cref{tab:predictor-error-and-task-distance,fig:predictor-error-by-horizon}):
the predictor degrades gracefully rather than collapsing to an uninformative guess. The
random-pair scale agrees with \cref{lem:random-pair}: with the measured mean radius 13.88
(\cref{app:gaussian-shell}), the lemma predicts a mean random-pair distance of
$\sqrt2\times13.88\approx19.63$, against the measured 19.6.

\begin{figure}[h]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig_predictor_error_by_horizon}
  \caption{Push-T predictor latent rollout error (blue) vs.\ horizon, against the random-pair
  reference distribution (gray).}
  \label{fig:predictor-error-by-horizon}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Task difficulty: how far apart are the actual start/goal pairs?} The random-pair
reference above (mean 19.6) is the scale at which two frames carry no information
about each other. Sampling same-episode (start, start$+H$) pairs the same way
\cref{fig:predictor-error-by-horizon} samples its rollout starts, but reading off the true
encoded distance $\|z_{\mathrm{start}}-z_{\mathrm{start}+H}\|$ instead of rolling the predictor
forward (\cref{tab:predictor-error-and-task-distance}), shows that 
$H=50$/$H=100$ are close to indistinguishable from random-pairs (19.4, 19.6) -- consistent with
\cref{sec:problem}'s Spearman collapse past $H{=}50$. Relative
to that task scale, the predictor's own rollout error is at 14.1\% of the gap at $H=25$ and
grows to 39.8\% by $H=100$ -- a sizeable fraction of the very distance CEM is trying
to optimize over, consistent with L2's success falling as its plans reach further ahead (\cref{tab:replan-frequency-appendix}).

