Success is non-monotonic in plan length, with the best results at 25--50 steps. Committing to
100 steps reduces L2's success by 7.6 points at same-ep 100 and 9.6 points at cross-ep, which is
consistent with predictor error compounding over a long open-loop chunk. Shorter plans perform
worse still. With a single-block lookahead the planner becomes greedy and cannot plan the
multi-step approach required before the block moves. At long range, the 50-step setting is within seed
variation of the default. The default 25-step plan length is at or near the best setting for L2, and the
gaps reported in \cref{tab:main} do not arise from the choice of plan lengths.

\subsection{Why a straight latent line goes out of distribution}
\label{app:gaussian-shell}
\Cref{app:predictor-error} shows the predictor itself degrades gracefully with horizon; this
section asks why the terminal L2 objective built on top of it still fails past $H{\approx}50$
(elite-ranking Spearman 0.396 $\to$ 0.086, \cref{app:cem-trace}). By \cref{prop:shell}, the
mass of a high-dimensional isotropic Gaussian lies on a thin shell of radius about
$\sigma\sqrt d$. If LeWM's Push-T latent cloud approximates such a shell, a straight line drawn between two points
\emph{on} the shell passes through the ball's interior, which the shell distribution assigns
almost no mass -- exactly the regime a $z$-space-L2 CEM plan implicitly cuts through when it
scores a candidate purely by terminal distance to a goal many steps away.

\paragraph{The latent cloud is close to an isotropic shell.} LeWM's SIGReg term regularises
the encoder towards an isotropic Gaussian, so the theory above should apply to its latents
directly. We test this on encoded Push-T frames ($d=192$). The latents are
centred: their empirical mean has norm 0.59, against a typical latent norm of 13.9, so we take
$\mu=0$. With $\sigma^2=\mathrm{tr}(\Sigma)/d$ the average per-dimension variance, the theory
predicts that $\|z\|$ follows $\sigma\chi_d$, with mean 13.88. The encoded frames match this
almost exactly (empirical mean 13.89, \cref{fig:gaussian-shell-r-hist}).

\begin{figure}[h]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.62\linewidth]{fig_gaussian_shell_r_hist}
  \caption{Push-T: norm $\|z\|$ of encoded frames (histogram of 200{,}000 of the 2.3M frames)
vs.\ the $\sigma\chi_{192}$ distribution predicted for a zero-mean isotropic Gaussian with the same
per-dimension variance ($\sigma=1.003$). Empirical mean 13.89 (sd 0.82); predicted mean 13.88
(sd 0.71).}
  \label{fig:gaussian-shell-r-hist}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{A straight-line midpoint is far off the shell; the real trajectory is not.} For 200{,}000
same-episode frame pairs 100 steps apart (the same-ep-100 horizon), both endpoints sit on the
shell as expected (start mean 14.15, goal mean 13.60), and so does the actual logged frame at the
trajectory's true midpoint (mean 13.81). The linear interpolation
$\tfrac12(z_{\mathrm{start}}+z_{\mathrm{goal}})$ does not: its mean radius is 10.03, 5.42
shell-$\sigma$ below the shell mean, and 96.4\% of the 200{,}000 interpolated midpoints fall below
the shell's own 1st percentile radius (\cref{fig:gaussian-shell-interp} in \cref{sec:problem}). The two populations do
not overlap. This isolates the effect to interpolation itself, not to trajectory midpoints being
unusual in general: the real dynamics stay on the shell, curving around it rather than cutting
through its interior, which is exactly the structure a graph built in a temporal-distance
representation is meant to follow instead of a raw latent straight line
(\cref{sec:method,sec:main}).



\section{Candidate ranking within CEM's own elites}
\label{app:cem-trace}
The ranking comparison in \cref{sec:analysis} is performed on candidates drawn from the planner's
own search rather than on a fixed diagnostic pool. This appendix defines the procedure.

\paragraph{Candidate set.} For each task $i$, with start state $s_0^{(i)}$ and goal observation
$o_g^{(i)}$, we run the first CEM solve of OUR and stop after iteration 1. At this iteration
both costs are evaluated on the same population of $N=300$ action sequences, sampled from
CEM's default initialisation $\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, I)$ in normalised action space. No elite
selection has yet shaped this population, so it is unbiased with respect to either cost. Let
$\mathcal{E}_i = \{a^{(i,1)}, \dots, a^{(i,K)}\}$ be the $K=30$ elites selected by OUR's cost.
Each $a^{(i,k)}$ is a 25-step sequence of raw actions, obtained by de-normalising and clipping
to the action bounds.

\paragraph{Costs.} For every $a \in \mathcal{E}_i$, the frozen predictor produces a single
imagined rollout. We evaluate two costs on this rollout: OUR's planning cost
$c_{\mathrm{OUR}}(a)$ (the complete objective of \cref{sec:method}), and LeWM's terminal cost
$c_{\mathrm{L2}}(a) = \lVert \hat z_H(a) - z_g \rVert_2^2$. The two costs therefore differ
only in the criterion, not in the predicted trajectory.

\paragraph{Oracle label.} Each elite is executed in the simulator from $s_0^{(i)}$, reaching
the true state $s^{(i,k)}$. From $s^{(i,k)}$, a privileged CEM planner with access to
the true simulator (population 64, 5 iterations, 2 independent trials) searches for the
smallest number of steps after which the environment's success predicate holds for goal
$o_g^{(i)}$. The recovery time is
\begin{equation}
  T^{(i,k)} = \min\bigl\{\, t \le T_{\max} : \text{the oracle reaches the goal in } t \text{ steps} \,\bigr\},
\end{equation}
where the minimum is taken over trials, and $T^{(i,k)} = T_{\max}+1$ if no trial succeeds
within the cap $T_{\max}=100$. Such labels are right-censored: they indicate that no plan was
found, not that recovery is impossible. Lower $T$ indicates a better candidate. The oracle
uses the simulator state and is not available to either planner.

\paragraph{Statistic.} For each task we compute the Spearman rank correlation
$\rho_i(c) = \operatorname{Spearman}\bigl(\{c(a^{(i,k)})\}_{k=1}^{K}, \{T^{(i,k)}\}_{k=1}^{K}\bigr)$
for $c \in \{c_{\mathrm{OUR}}, c_{\mathrm{L2}}\}$. A positive value means that the cost assigns lower values to
candidates that recover faster. Tasks on which all $K$ labels are equal (typically all
censored) have undefined $\rho_i$ and are excluded. We report the mean
$\bar\rho(c) = |\mathcal{I}|^{-1}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{I}} \rho_i(c)$ over the set $\mathcal{I}$ of
remaining tasks, with 95\% confidence intervals from a bootstrap over tasks.

\paragraph{Setup.} We use 25 tasks from each task pool, CEM seed 0, and learned-asset seed 0. Because $\mathcal{E}_i$ is selected by
$c_{\mathrm{OUR}}$, the comparison measures how well each cost orders the candidates that OUR
would retain. It does not measure how well each cost ranks the full population.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering\small
\caption{Mean per-task Spearman correlation $\bar\rho$ between each cost and the oracle recovery
time $T$ over OUR's $K=30$ elites at CEM iteration 1. $|\mathcal{I}|$ is the number of tasks with
non-degenerate labels. At same-ep 100, 14 of 25 tasks were fully censored even with a larger
oracle cap.}
\label{tab:cem-trace-rho}
\begin{tabular}{lcrr}
\toprule
protocol & $|\mathcal{I}|$ & $\bar\rho(c_{\mathrm{OUR}})$ & $\bar\rho(c_{\mathrm{L2}})$ \\
\midrule
same-ep 25  & 25 & 0.327  & 0.396 \\
same-ep 50  & 24 & 0.203  & 0.086 \\
same-ep 100 & 11 & 0.191  & $-$0.064 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Result.} At same-ep 25, $c_{\mathrm{L2}}$ has the higher mean correlation. At same-ep 50 and 100, $c_{\mathrm{OUR}}$ has the
higher correlation. The L2 ranking at these horizons is not distinguishable
from chance. The crossover matches the short-range/long-range pattern of closed-loop success in
\cref{tab:main}, here measured on the candidate ranking of a single CEM iteration.

